let's talk about anti-semitism i've  talked about right-wing and left-wing  anti-semitism before and many of us on  the left are quick to admit that  anti-semitism on the right is a major  problem  and it is a major problem and i've  talked about it before right right-wing  anti-semitism from  white nationalists the patriot and  militia type movements  many sources of right-wing anti-semitism  but we're also seeing left-wing  anti-semitism and it's coming from  different places it's sometimes coming  on college campuses  it's sometimes from so-called  african-american  rights groups that we've covered in the  past anti-semitic statements and  and uh uh um uh i guess statements is  what i what i would really call it  proclamations of sorts  and the causes of left-wing  anti-semitism are different  than the causes on the right and this  isn't a comprehensive list  but left-wing anti-semitism sometimes  stems from  antipathy towards israeli foreign or  domestic policy  it can stem from the perception that  despite being one of the most persecuted  groups in human history jews are now  seen by some as a privileged group in  some countries  and sometimes as a group undeserving of  the so-called privilege  that they're perceived to have sometimes  it's related to straight-up conspiracy  theories of control  sometimes it's merely the feeling that  in order to be for palestinian rights  you have to be more vocally against jews  at large  there's many reasons that i haven't  mentioned and it's also combinations of  the reasons that i have  in any case college campuses are quickly  becoming an epicenter  of left-wing anti-semitism and there  have been some horrible incidents over  the last week or so  recently there was an event called the  chicago dyke march  on saturday it's an lgbt rights event  and three people  carrying jewish pride flags in other  words it's  it's a rainbow lgbtq flag  with the star of david in the middle  basically saying hey the jewish  community supports lgbtq rights  they were asked to leave the march  because they quote repeatedly expressed  support for zionism which is a by and  large  pretty meaningless phrase and one member  of the dyke march collective said that  those people carrying the rainbow flags  with the star of david in the middle  were total leaves because people to  leave because people were feeling  unsafe as a result of the flags adding  that the march was  an anti-zionist pro-palestinian march  according to the windy city times and  one of those people who was kicked out  of the march said  she was approached multiple times about  her flag this is laurel grower  who said they were telling me to leave  because my flag was a trigger  to people that they found offensive  a member of the dyke march collective  spoke to the chicagoist on sunday  and insisted oh no our organization  isn't anti-semitic  but we don't want to be associated with  anything that could be interpreted as  pro-israel  and the dyke march organizers  subsequently released a statement  explaining that the decision to remove  jewish marchers was quote made after  they  repeatedly expressed support for zionism  during conversations with dyke march  collective members  what was one of those expressions of  zionism  well laurel grower said that she  believes in a two-state solution  to the israeli-palestinian conflict and  apparently that was just  too much listen uh infighting  is a destructive force in social  movements and the lgbtq movement  as has been exposed by a number of good  investigative pieces  is having an issue with anti-semitic  elements and it's particularly  self-destructive  and disturbing to see groups fighting  for minority rights  often reject the support of jews in the  black community  which in some places is directly at odds  with jews for example  near where i used to live in brooklyn in  south williamsburg brooklyn in new york  city  there is pretty significant tension  between the hasidic and black  communities there  and it's extra upsetting to me to see  that giving the long  long very long history in the united  states of jews being there  fighting for minority rights lgbtq  rights  african-american rights mississippi  burning right many people don't know  this  mississippi burning the movie based on  the 1964 murders  of three civil rights workers during the  civil rights movement  two of the three people killed there  were jews and  historically jewish groups have been at  the forefront of fighting for lgbt  rights groups like the anti-defamation  league  this is so toxic and so destructive and  at the core whatever your feelings are  pat  movements that start this infighting end  up  mostly hurting themselves it really is  too bad that this is going on  on the left uh if you look at the other  side republicans have had a big issue  with racism ever since  lyndon johnson signed the south away  with the civil rights act and the voting  rights act sure  now of course that's not indicative of  every republican and it's small in  numbers but still very significant  sure and that's why i think um you know  conservatives do have a responsibility  to call  their fellow racist conservatives out on  that as do we and if  this continues to grow we'll have that  res that same responsibility when it  comes to anti-semitism on the left  couldn't agree more couldn't agree more  and this is just i mean  what are we gaining what what did the  lgbtq rights  pride movement gain by kicking someone  out  for having a flag that indicated the  jewish community supports lgbtq rights  and believed in a two-state solution to  the israeli-palestinian conflict explain  to me  how the march was better off okay
 

